# Ford Pilot V8 four-door saloon, 1947-1951.
#
# The first large post-war British Ford, on the pre-war 22 hp Model 62
# chassis with the 3,622 cc side-valve V8: separate flowing wings, exposed
# running boards, an upright flat windscreen with quarter lights and a short
# boot. 22,155 built. The estate, pick-up, panel van and Australian utility
# are different bodies and are not modelled.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Pilot
#
# Dimensions 4.445 x 1.765 m, wheelbase 2.750 m. Height was not established
# from a published source; the model's height comes from the photographs.
#
# Shape reference: a green Pilot in true level side elevation (800 px only),
# a 1949 car in front three-quarter, and a rear three-quarter. Image 1
# governs the wings, running boards and roofline; the resolution limits fine
# detail, not the proportions.
#
# Colours: grade (d). No Pilot colour chart was found. The photographs show
# green, black-over-grey and black cars; plain names are used, not invented
# period names. Weights 3, 2, 2, 1, total 8, provisional.
#
# Speed 130 km/h. Comfort 45/398, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 45: a roomy but
# upright and hard-riding 1940s saloon. Classes 2,3.
# Dates are road presence: 1947 intro, retires staggered 1965-1968.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/ford-pilot.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/ford-pilot.dae. References:
# citycars/references/ford-pilot/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the wing-to-running-board transitions are too abrupt and
# the spare-wheel cover projects too far. The model height of 1.67 m is an
# estimate, the published height not having been established. At 128 px the
# separate wings, upright grille and short boot read as a Pilot.


